          "solution": "The paradox happens because: (1) Seasonal concentration: Most water arrives in 3–4 monsoon months. The other 8–9 months are dry. Rivers are full then empty. (2) Uneven distribution: The Ganges basin gets abundant rain; Thar Desert gets little. Not all regions are equal. (3) Population mismatch: Population is not distributed evenly with water. North India has rivers but also has most of India's people. Monsoon-dependent South faces periodic droughts. (4) Pollution: Many rivers (Ganges, Yamuna) are heavily polluted. Available water is unsafe to use. (5) Inefficiency: Irrigation systems lose 40–50% of water to evaporation and leakage. Agriculture uses 80% of water but is inefficient. (6) Climate change: Irregular monsoons, glacial melt, and warming are unpredictable. Some years have floods; others have drought. Solutions: (a) Dams and reservoirs to store excess monsoon water for dry season. (b) Watershed protection to increase water retention. (c) Groundwater regulation. (d) Irrigation efficiency (drip irrigation). (e) Pollution control. (f) Water harvesting. The lesson: having rivers is not enough; managing them wisely is the challenge."
